      ],
      "text": "Mark Rowe investigates attempts to reintroduce elms to Britain\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        450.571044921875,
        516.1417846679688,
        628.43359375
      ],
      "text": "A. Around 25 million elms, accounting for 90% of all elm trees in the UK, died\nduring the 1960s and '70s of Dutch elm disease. In the aftermath, the elm,\nonce so dominant in the British landscape, was largely forgotten. However,\nthere's now hope the elm may be reintroduced to the countryside of central\nand southern England. Any reintroduction will start from a very low base. 'The\nimpact of the disease is difficult to picture if you hadn't seen what was there\nbefore,' says Matt Elliot of the Woodland Trust. 'You look at old photographs\nfrom the 1960s and it's only then that you realise the impact [elms had] ...\nThey were significant, large trees... then they were gone.'\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        641.3710327148438,
        511.5699462890625,
        759.713623046875
      ],
      "text": "B. The disease is caused by a fungus that blocks the elms' vascular (water,\nnutrient and food transport) system, causing branches to wilt and die. A first\nepidemic, which occurred in the 1920s, gradually died down, but in the '70s a\nsecond epidemic was triggered by shipments of elm from Canada. The wood\ncame in the form of logs destined for boat building and its intact bark was\nperfect for the elm bark beetles that spread the deadly fungus. This time, the\n"
